e analyze this Turn situation (4 cards on board, 1 more to come).\n\n**My hand:** 10♠ 9♥\n**Board:** 7♠ 2♦ 8♥ 9♣\n\n**Current hand strength:** I have a pair of 9s (middle pair). More importantly, I have an open-ended straight draw (6-7-8-9-10 would make a straight if a 6 or Jack comes on the river).\n\n**Outs analysis:**\n- Any 6 gives me a straight (4 outs)\n- Any Jack gives me a straight (4 outs)\n- Any 10 gives me two pair (3 outs, since I have 10♠)\n- Total: ~11 strong outs\n\nWith 1 card to come, 11 outs gives me roughly 11/46 ≈ 24% chance to improve to a strong hand.\n\n**Pot situation:** \n- Both players have checked through pre-flop and flop\n- Pot is small (just blinds: 200 chips)\n- Both players still have nearly full stacks (~20,000)\n\n**Strategic considerations:**\n1. My pair of 9s might be ahead currently, but it's vulnerable\n2. My straight draw is strong with ~24% equity to improve\n3. Checking has shown weakness from both sides - no one has a monster\n4. A small bet could build the pot for when I hit my draw, or take it down now\n5. If I check, I see the river for free but give opponent a free card too\n\n**Opponent's likely holdings:** Given the checking, opponent likely has a weak pair, weak draw, or high cards that missed. They don't have a strong hand or they would have bet.\n\n**Strategic options:**\n- **Check (0):** Safe, see free river, but no fold equity and don't build pot\n- **Small raise (100-500):** Builds pot, might take it down, maintains some chips if called\n- **Larger raise:** Too aggressive with just a draw and weak pair\n- **Fold:** Unnecessary - I have equity and showdown value\n\nReason:\nI choose to make a small bet of 100 chips. This accomplishes several goals: (1) it builds the pot for when I potentially hit my straight or improve to two pair, (2) it gives me fold equity against weak hands or missed draws, (3) it's a small enough risk that I'm not committed if raised, and (4) it takes initiative after passive play. With 11 ou